From 6b10af24c6e8747168d2dc5c26a24f56fb142c13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Steven Huang <shuang@myai001.mylab.io>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:17:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] perf(sm75): decode e4m3 weights with integer ops
 instead of the payload LUT

The 256-entry payload LUT looked up every fp8 weight byte in a gather
that hits up to 32 distinct L1 sectors per warp instruction, and that L1
traffic serializes against the weight stream. On a 4096x4096 GEMV the
kernel is latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound, and removing only the LUT
measured 283 -> 450 GB/s.

_e4m3_to_f16 replaces it with a shift and an add: for a normal e4m3 the
fp16 bits are the exponent field moved for the bias change (7 -> 15)
with the mantissa seven bits up, and e4m3fn is finite so the carry case
cannot reach the exponent field. Subnormals are a three-bit integer
times 2^-9. Exact on every non-NaN byte, which the checkpoint has none
of, so the dequantized weights are bit-identical to the LUT path.

Wired into the narrow GEMV (the decode path) only; the wide kernel's
deep-grid tiles are issue-bound and lose 0.59-0.80x to the integer ops
there (measured at q_b and the LM head shard), so it keeps the gather.
SGLANG_SM75_W8A16_ALU_DECODE=0 is the A/B switch. Interleaved A/B on the
running engine: all SM75 flags off 24.5-25.1 tok/s vs on 26.8-27.3.
