From 331faaeaf794541fcb6d1615a7ad1a3293ac50d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Steven Huang <shuang@myai001.mylab.io>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 23:37:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] feat(sm75): add sparse-index validation and launch-sync
 diagnostics

Two switches, both off by default, that were needed to attribute an async
illegal memory access to a launch instead of to whichever eager kernel
happened to run next. Kept because the next occurrence will need them again,
and because the alternative -- rediscovering that CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING does
not cover Triton's cuLaunchKernel -- costs a day.

SGLANG_VALIDATE_SPARSE_INDICES checks the sparse-decode page indices against
[0, num_pages * page_size) on the host, so a stale entry is named instead of
being allowed to fault later. It clamps rather than raises, and costs a device
sync per call, so it is a debugging tool only.

SGLANG_TRITON_SYNC_EVERY_LAUNCH synchronizes the device after each Triton
launch. This is what produced the two trustworthy stacks that closed the IMA
investigation; the report is still one launch late, which is the floor for this
technique. It serializes the device, so throughput numbers collected with it on
are meaningless -- every measurement arm in this series ran with it off.
